From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jaypark <jaypark@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:08:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Let the database refuse what is taken

A board's name and a post's title on its board are taken once. Until
now the views asked first, and 0011 said what that leaves open: two
requests at once both ask, both find it free, and the unique constraint
refuses the second as a 500. Now the write itself is the question.

- board_name_taken.sql and post_title_taken.sql go, and with them
  0011's questions and 0031's skip_id?. The views write, and hand the
  error to the form:

      b, err := InsertBoard(ctx, tx, NewBoardInput{Name: f.Name.Value})
      if f.Fail(err) {
          return maago.Show(BoardFormPage{f}), nil
      }
      if err != nil {
          return maago.Result[BoardFormPage]{}, err
      }

  maago gen gives every form Fail(err). When err is the model's unique
  refusing the row, it writes the message on the one field of that
  constraint the form has (on the form itself if it has none or more
  than one) and is true; otherwise it is false and err is the view's to
  return. Board.Name is that field for a board. Post's unique=(BoardID,Title) has only Title on the form, so
  the message goes on Title
- savePost does the same after InsertPost, and APICreatePost stays as
  it is: it turns what savePost wrote on f.Title.Error into a 422
- ChangePost gets a case after maago.ErrConflict:

      case f.Fail(err): // the title is taken on this board

  skip_id is not needed: an UPDATE that keeps the post's own title
  writes the same row, and the constraint does not ring
- The transaction goes on after Fail. The generated InsertX and UpdateX
  write inside a savepoint, so on PostgreSQL a refused row does not
  abort the request's transaction: the page still reads, and commits
- Two notices pinned at once have no constraint to refuse them, so
  PinnedOverlap stays a question asked first, before the write, so now
  before the title too: a post whose title and pin both clash shows the
  pin's error
- The message is the framework's, as "This field is required." is:
  "This value is already taken." Config.Messages.Taken changes it. The
  messages the board wrote itself (Validate's, the pin's, the
  conflict's) stay as they are

The tests see the new message where they saw the old ones: the same
board name, the same title on a board, through the API, and on edit.
TestWriteRace sends two at once: a transaction writes a name and holds
on, a request writes the same name (waiting at the unique index if it
gets there first), and the first commits. The request gets its form back with the message
and goes on to commit its page, the race 0011 left open now refused by
the database.

diff --git a/board/queries/board_name_taken.sql b/board/queries/board_name_taken.sql
deleted file mode 100644
index e2f58cf..0000000
--- a/board/queries/board_name_taken.sql
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,7 +0,0 @@
--- name: BoardNameTaken :one Count
--- How many boards have the name: 0 or 1.
--- param: name string
--- column: n int64
-SELECT count(*) AS n
-FROM board_board
-WHERE name = :name
diff --git a/board/queries/post_title_taken.sql b/board/queries/post_title_taken.sql
deleted file mode 100644
index e17b664..0000000
--- a/board/queries/post_title_taken.sql
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,11 +0,0 @@
--- name: PostTitleTaken :one Count
--- How many posts of the board have the title: 0 or 1. With skip_id, the
--- post being changed does not count.
--- param: board_id int64
--- param: title    string
--- param: skip_id? int64
--- column: n int64
-SELECT count(*) AS n
-FROM board_post
-WHERE board_id = :board_id AND title = :title
-  {skip_id} AND id <> :skip_id
